<blockquote>
Technology Corner: Story of a New Computer<br />
Written by <a href="mailto:mayhem@conclaveofguilds.com">Mayhem</a><br />
<br />
For our final article of July, we're introducing the Technology series.  The
articles within this category will always focus on the hardware and software
used for gaming.  Since this is the first installment of the series, and
since I know many gamers are getting ready for the release of EQII, I
figured I'd share with you my story of starting from scratch and buying a
system.  As a brief aside, I reside in The Netherlands thus the prices I
paid are a) unlikely to be representitive of the market in your own country,
and b) expressed in euros, which when converted to dollars is usually within
10%, give or take.<br />
<br />
The hardest part for me was setting a budget.  As I'm getting married in
February 2004, the discussion of spending money on a system was long, drawn
out, and hard-fought.  After all, buying a system is equivalent to paying
for most of an open-bar party for 100 guests!  While you may not be getting
married, we all have things that need paying for.  Thus budgetting for a new
system becomes important.<br />
<br />
Originally I wanted a 2500 euro budget.  This would have bought a very
comfortable (nnot to mention pretty) high-end system.  After receiving a
counter offer of around 800 euros, there was much debate and a savings plan
was instituted.  I was allowed to buy whatever I could save for by my
birthday in September.  Well, this didn't work out either.  After much
debate and haggling, the end result is a system which will come in at right
around 1100 euros.  This is, in the eyes of my partner, a much more (though
not perfectly so) reasonable amount.  (I honestly have to agree with her,
much as I'd like a disgustingly fast system, I also want a nice wedding!)
Of course, nothing ever goes completely to plan, and the end result (after a
few mistakes and corrections) is a 1200 euro system that has pretty decent
potential even in the mid-term.<br />
<br />
So, making a lower-budget full system, what do you do?  Well, identify the
core components you "need" and try to make the best cost-to-performance
decisions you can of course!  In this case, planning a system for gaming,
and in specific Everquest II, the power of the system in handling video was
very important.  Five major components influence this, and thus they
received the most attention.  The other components were very much given a
backseat.<br />
<br />
The first and most vital of all components was deemed the video card itself.
At the present time, there are essentially five basic cards available for
consideration: The (ATI) Radeon 9800 Pro, Radeon 9700 Pro, Radeon 9600 Pro,
(NVIDIA) geForce FX 5600, and geForce FX5900.  There are some subdivisions
within the cards based upon memory, added features and such, but for the
most part these five are the most commonly available.  Multiple
manufacturers are available for all of these chipsets.  The manufacturer
dictates a number of things about the card, and some cards will perform
slightly better or worse in areas such as fan noise or software bundlling,
but for the most part will be capable of the same basic performance
levels.<br />
<br />
When making the decision (second hardest, mind you) as to which of these
cards to go for, I had to really ponder carefully.  Though the different in
performance between the Radeon 9600 Pro (averaging around 195 euros) and the
geForce FX 5600 (averaging around 180 euros) is minimal, the difference
between, the price was a clear influence in the direction of the GeForce
card.  However, when taking the likely strain of Everquest II into account,
it was apparent that a serious card would be required.  Eliminating these
two mid-level cards, I looked upwards to the other three.  The Radeon 9800
Pro and geForce FX 5900 perfom admirably as top-of-the-line cards should.
Unfortunately they both have a price to match.  The best decision for my
situation turned out to be the Radeon 9700 Pro, which benchmarks show offers
twice the directX (UT2003) framerate of either the 9600 Pro or FX 5600 when
running with Image Quality enabled in high resolutions.  While not as
powerful as either the 9800 PRO (which has the best image quality available
currently) or FX 5900 (the leader in pure speed), it does perform very
respectably (achieving perhaps 80% of what the other cards do) and seems a
good investment.  The card available to me (GigaByte Maya II GV-R9700 PRO)
was around 315 euros, representing a considerable portion (30%) of my
budget.<br />
<br />
After having spent such a large amount of the budget on the video card, I
needed to find an affordable display.  In order to enjoy high resolution
gaming, a large monitor is a must.  I had my heard set on a flat CRT 19"
monitor, and managed to pour over specs and prices until I found the one
which fit my requirements, as well as my budget.  Higher end (aperature
grill) 19" monitors start in the low 300 euro range, which while preferable
from a clarity standpoint were just too expensive to make the cut.  The
Hansol 920P is a shadow mask flat square tube 19" monitor capable of a
maximum resolution and refresh rate of 1600x1200 at 75hz.  It was a very
affordable 160 euros (!), and if it performs to specs it should be a good
match for the video card.  What is important to remember when buying a
monitor is that you should be purchasing one you intend to use for three to
five years.<br />
<br />
With the core of my display taken care of, I went on to make the agonizing
(because there are so many powerful chips available) decision regarding
which processor to buy.  The leader of price-to-performance in the budget
range continues to be AMD.  Scouring benchmarks and pricelists the obvious
conclusion is the Athlon XP 2700+.  Running at just over 2ghz, this set me
back 160 (retail boxed with fan) euros, but it offers a noticable
improvement in power over the lower speed Athlon XP chips, without the leap
in price of the higher-end ones.  The performance of a 2700+ is just below
that of a Pentium 2.4ghz in most benchmarks, and the price difference (200
euros for the Pentium) sealed the matter.  After discussing the cooling
issues of the Athlon XP line of processors with a friend, I also ordered a
Thermaltake Volcano 9+ fan.  This should enable me to do a bit of
overclocking as well, since it's always nice to push the system a bit.<br />
<br />
Every processor needs somewhere to hang out, so I found myself pouring over
the dozens of motherboards for one which called out to me.  It was important
to me to acquire a board which supported dual DDR 400 RAM, thus I checked
various nForce chipset boards.  In the end it came down to price.  Though
only a couple of euros cheaper than similar boards (88 euros), I chose the
Chaintech 7NJL1 Apogee.  The board features dual DDR400, 10/100 LAN, and 6
channel audio.  Though a bit shy on features, it has the core I needed, and
will take the Athlon XP 3200+ should I wish to upgrade in the future.  It is
worth noting that the other prohibiting factor in moving into the Pentium
market is the fact that the prices of P4 motherboards supporting the modern
line of chips exceed those supporting the Athlon by a considerable margin.
Overall the move to a Pentium base would have been nearly 100 euros more.
The capability for future expansion would have been greater, thus if you
have the money I would suggest it.<br />
<br />
Adding memory to the system was actually a difficult thing, in part due to
the fact that I made the decision to go with the nForce-based mainboard.
With 3 DIMM slots, the dual DDR 400 feature is supported only on two slots.
Thus, unlike the Pentium boards, once you buy your RAM, you're a bit stuck.
Originally I thought that I would just go with 512mb of RAM, however after
careful consideration and realizing that I may actually want/need more
before the lifespan of the system is up, I chose to buy two 512mb DDR 400
(PC3200) sticks of RAM.  Of course, this decision came with a caveat, and
that was that I couldn't afford a brand name.  Thus my RAM is of the OEM
variety, and is likely to suffer a performance hit (and has a slight chance
of not working in dual channel setup) as a result.  On the bright side, a
full gigabyte of DDR 400 only cost me 165 euros.  However, if you can afford
it you should buy your RAM in tested pairs.  Many manufacturers now offer
this type of package, and it saves the risk of your RAM sticks not working
together, as well as generally giving a small performance increase.<br />
<br />
Having debated going for a cheap case (I found one for 31 euros) I made the
final decision to go with something which had more cooling capacity (the
cheap case had no fans or mounts) as well as a solid power supply.  Antec is
a quality case and power supply name, so I went with an Antec WS SX835 II EC
case including a 350W "SmartPower" power supply.  While "underpowered" for a
truly burly system, the 350W should provide ample support so long as I don't
overload it with too many devices in the future.  This case features a roomy
design as well as two installed fans and space for two more.  Taking
advantage of this, I threw in one extra 80mm fan to increase airflow from
front to back.  A cheap upgrade, but worth it when given the heat generated
by modern components.  It should be a nice enclosure with enough airflow to
enable overclocking the video card and processor to squeeze out a bit more
performance.<br />
<br />
For storage I was torn between my desire to keep the price down, and my
price-to-gig (I remember when I used to think in price-to-meg) mentality.
While evaluating my options, I came to rest on the Maxtor DiamondMax Plus 9
80GB UATA-133 drive.  While I could have saved some money on a cheaper
drive, I would have been paying about 50 eurocents more per gigabyte.  Thus
I bit the bullet and spent the 90 euros, which works out to barely over a
euro per gigabyte for a 7200RPM drive.<br />
<br />
Choosing to use a CD-ROM drive instead of a burner would have saved a bit of
money, but being able to burn MP3s for use on the new car stereo was too
important to sacrifice.  Since there is very little difference in the
quality between burners (at least at the low end) I went with a Samsung
52x24x52x model based upon it having the lowest (46 euro) price.  I could
have saved a bit less than two euros if I'd gone for a slower burner, but it
seemed rather foolish.<br />
<br />
Since I use a dialup connection (for at least another six months it would
seem) I chose a 56k modem with the lowest price tag I could find.  I expect
no problems from this decision, as 56k technology is many years old now.
The Sweex modem was 15 euros and should do the job until I get broadband.<br
/>
<br />
Again in an effort to save a bit of money I chose a headset which wasn't too
expensive.  The Sweex "deluxe" headset is actually a US Blaster model, and
incorporates large earphones with a boom mike.  Spending 13 euros for this
wasn't as cheap as I could have gone, but it does provide a large set of
earpads which I personally prefer over smaller models.<br />
<br />
The final component (I'm cheating by re-using my Logitech Trackman Marble
Cordless as an input device) I needed to buy was a keyboard.  Not wanting to
invest in anything amazing at this point, I settled on a 6 euro offering
from Sweex.  Non-ergonomic, of course, but "real gamers" prefer that anyway!
It should "do the job" and if it doesn't I'll make it into a piece of modern
art.<br />
<br />
Overall the system weighs in with enough power that it should be able to
handle Everquest II reasonably well.  Sure, there will be quite a few
options which may need to be disabled, but I expect the quality to be
adequate, and hope the system will last for a decent period of time.  The
processor may be upgraded still, as may the video card of course.  In
addition, a sound card could be installed along with some decent speakers.
But most importantly, I'm not going to be stuck working on a Pentium (1!)
133mhz laptop anymore.  Not that I'm downing the laptop, it deserves
countless decorations for the service it has provided!  All that remains now
is for me to wait until the order comes in, then manage to put the beast
together.<br />
<br />
The final list of components and prices:<br />
<br />
1x GigaByte Radeon 9700 Pro w/128Mb DDR - 314.16E<br />
1x Hansol 920P CRT 19" FST Monitor - 160.65E<br />
1x AMD Athlon XP 2700+, 333Mhz, SocketA, Retail Box - 159.46E<br />
1x Thermaltake Volcano 9+ Fan - 24.99E<br />
1x Chaintech 7NJL1 Apogee nForce Mainboard - 88.06E<br />
2x DDR 512Mb PC3200 OEM Memory - 164.22E<br />
1x Antec WS SX835 II EC 350Watt Case - 113.05E<br />
1x Antec Casefan 80mm - 7.14E<br />
1x Maxtor DiamondMax Plus 9, 80Gb, 7200rpm U-ATA133 HDD - 89.25E<br />
1x Samsung CD-Rewriter 52x24x52x IDE Bulk - 46.41E<br />
1x Sweex 56K hardware PCI modem - 14.51E<br />
1x SWEEX USBlaster 360  Stereo Headphones w/Mic - 12.50E<br />
1x Sweex SW-10 Professional Keyboard - 5.80E<br />
<br />
Grand total: 1200.20E<br />
<br />
(NOTE: In order to run a system you must have an input device of some kind,
as well as the all-important operating system.  I have these things already
and thus did not need to buy them.  Adding them in would have brough my
system up to somewhere between 1300 and 1400 euros.)<br />
<br />
In the next issue of Technology we'll be taking a closer look on the video
components available, as well as those which are expected later on this
year.  After all, Everquest II should be quite a good-looking game, why not
get ready to enjoy it?<br />
<br />
</blockquote>


